WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:08.400
The protests that have consumed Hong Kong for more than three months started because of a proposed bill

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:14.560
that would have made it easier for mainland China to extradite citizens from this semi-autonomous city.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.140
Violent clashes between protesters and police culminated in a confrontation

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:25.120
at a subway station on August 31st when police appeared to beat demonstrators with batons

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.880
possibly resulting in one fatality, though
authorities dispute that claim.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.880
The activists, politicians, and academics we spoke with

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.120
said that the protest movement has become about much more than the extradition bill,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.180
which the government has since withdrawn.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:46.420
It's a fight for the survival of an island of liberalism in the shadow of an increasingly authoritarian super state

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.480
with ambitions of global dominance.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:54.700
We are at the frontline between the greatest dictatorship in human history

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.880
which is CCP, and the free world.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.260
And Hongkongers, we've shown that we stand with the free world.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.260
And, of course, we hope the rest of the free world

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.320
can support us

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.540
in this battle

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.320
in this war between dictatorship

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.320
and freedom.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.220
Hong Kong's peculiar one country two systems governance model

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:22.080
began when Britain handed over its former colony to China in 1997

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:28.000
on the condition that it be allowed to maintain its existing governance structure for another half-century.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.420
This city of more than 7 million prospered economically with China mostly holding up its end of the bargain.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:38.260
Hong Kong's Constitution protects freedom of speech and assembly,

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:42.540
but the city enjoys what's been
described as liberty without democracy.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.920
The government, including the city's chief executive, isn't entirely elected by the people.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.940
If you did this interview with me in 1997 and asked me,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.980
when did you expect that people in Hong Kong would get this universal suffrage?

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:02.980
I think most people at the time would say, well maybe 10 years. That was almost like part of the promise,

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:07.520
in the basic law. But as time goes by, you know, promises were broken.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.800
Though he supports the universal suffrage, Charles Mok wasn't elected by popular vote

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.230
to his position in Hong Kong's legislature but rather selected by a smaller pool

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:23.520
by a smaller pool of stakeholders as an official representative of the city's tech sector.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.240
Like Mok, the majority of representatives didn't get there via the ballot box,

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.680
and not surprisingly most members of the legislature who were not voted in by the citizens

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.840
side with mainland China and not the protesters.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:43.820
As one journalist put it, Hong Kong is controlled by a conspiracy of local tycoons and mainland communists

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:49.500
colluding against the citizenry. So the citizenry has turned to the streets.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.440
Largely because we are not a democracy. Because the government is not elected

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:01.060
I think street level protests, largely peaceful, are actually important means for the Hong Kong people

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:02.780
to protest government actions.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.340
And protest has often worked.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:11.320
The 2014 Umbrella Movement led the government to withdraw a proposal to pre-select

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.851
a limited slate of presidential candidates.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.120
And the recent uprising led to the withdrawal of the extradition bill.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.900
The 2014 protests also spawned a mostly youth-led movement to increase democracy

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.480
as a way of preserving the city's autonomy.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:32.120
Joshua Wong was just 15 when he led a successful movement overturning a plan

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.300
to impose a pro Communist Party history curriculum in Hong Kong schools.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.760
He then started the political organization, Demosisto.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.020
Which has played a central role in the protest movement and even elected a candidate to office,

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:50.960
who, along with other youth activists elected in 2016, were later disqualified from serving.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:57.460
At their swearing-in ceremony last month Sixtus Leung and Yayoi Ching unfurled banners

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.080
calling for an independent Hong Kong.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.280
To people who support Hong Kong independence

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:02.780
logically

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.440
we cannot find a way out

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.900
One of the more controversial youth leaders is 'Sixtus' Baggio Leung

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.780
who is part of a right-leaning movement advocating for full independence from mainland China.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.700
I don't think Hong Kong can survive

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.460
if people around the world

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:20.980
don't trust our system

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.240
They don't trust

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.500
they will be safe

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.820
when they travel to Hong Kong

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.580
or do business in Hong Kong

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:29.940
Why would they come?

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.940
And why would they need us?

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.200
So to keep separation from China

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.100
keep a distance from China

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.960
to try to be more

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.180
of a natural state

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.620
is the best solution.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:49.700
But the problem of course is that there seems to be no institutional channel by which you can do this.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:56.220
And also I don't see, really tangible movements to actually make that into a reality.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.720
So a lot of that is just talk.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.760
Leung's pro-independence position is still considered radical in Hong Kong.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:08.680
And experts point out that Chinese state-controlled media often highlights independence activists

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:15.260
in an attempt to portray the protests as part of a radical separatist movement in need of violent suppression.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:21.600
They're using this sort of propaganda to cater to the domestic audience to justify

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.940
for the kind of force they use on Hong Kong.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:27.700
Do you think that strategy might backfire on China?

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:28.840
It is backfiring.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:32.920
More and more people in Hong Kong particularly a portion of the young people

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.320
are beginning to say that I'm only a Hong Konger.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:41.120
it's all because of this action reaction the more you push the more I want to break away.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.620
If Hong Kong remained

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:44.760
under the ruling of the Chinese Communist Party

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.460
we can never achieve universal suffrage

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.920
separation of power,

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.880
freedom of speech

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:53.960
all kids of freedom.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:10.720
We see the 96% of the entertainment scene silencing themselves

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.320
and really just trying to keep out of trouble.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:22.860
Pop singer Denise Ho is one of the few local celebrities who is publicly supporting the protesters.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:28.320
Others like Jackie Chan and Liu Yifei,
the star of Disney's live-action Mulan reboot,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.340
have praised the Hong Kong police and criticized the demonstrators for being too destructive.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:40.820
Right now we are at a point where the communist government they do not accept this stance of neutrality.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:45.600
For them staying silent means that you are with the protesters.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:52.220
They are trying to force everyone to choose a side. Most of the people they would be afraid to

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:58.280
go against this huge machine because everyone wants a piece of the Chinese market.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:00.760
Ho has been banned from performing in the mainland.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.680
She says her records have also been removed from Chinese stores,

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:08.360
and some of her international shows have been cancelled  because of alleged security concerns.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:12.800
Mainland China detained employees of a Hong Kong bookstore known for selling literature

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.980
critical of the Communist Party on dubious charges.

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:20.920
Prospective candidates with certain controversial views are barred from running for office.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.600
Leaders of the protests have been imprisoned,

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:28.780
and there's talk of making it illegal for Hong Kong residents to boo the Chinese national anthems

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.000
such as that this recent soccer match.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:41.640
The core values that we are safeguarding
are the freedom to speak our minds,

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:49.040
to take our political stance to protest without fear of being prosecuted.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:54.720
That has been something that has been given to us in
the earlier days as a British colony.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.060
A lot of Hong Kong people think that like
15 years ago

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.820
we had a higher level of freedom,

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:04.280
of freedom of speech or
various other aspects.

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:09.940
But now it seems that we can feel a Hong Kong becoming more and more like China.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:11.420
In terms of political values

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:14.200
I always believe that one of
the biggest differences between Hong Kong

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:21.160
and the Beijing government is the emphasis on rule of
law, freedom of speech, human rights

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:25.140
and also, maybe, to a lesser extent
democracy.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:34.160
Activists see the fight for more democracy as a path to securing more Liberty.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:40.040
They're demanding the citizens be allowed to fully elect the city's legislative body and chief executive.

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:44.790
But winning that right
requires approval from Beijing.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:49.020
It's really not only about Hong Kong and
Hong Kong government

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.980
or the communist government behind it,

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:55.120
it's really a fight of these universal values

00:08:55.120 --> 00:09:02.240
that we as a more hybrid city might be aligned with a
lot of different Western societies

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.560
and then the set of communist values that
the Chinese government

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:11.040
and maybe even other authoritarian governments around the world

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.500
they are trying to bring into the world with their powers.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:18.000
Personally I feel this is a very global fight

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.980
of  these two different sets of values.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:24.340
The activists are also appealing for help from the US.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:27.440
Denise Ho, Joshua Wong of Demosisto

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:33.620
and some other pro-democracy activists appeared before a U.S. Congressional committee on Sept 17th

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:39.000
and urged US lawmakers to
pass the Hong Kong Human Rights and Democracy Act,

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.600
which would make the city's bilateral trade and travel agreements with America

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.900
contingent on China maintaining Hong Kong's autonomy.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:48.840
It would also levy sanctions against
Chinese individuals

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.380
accused of violating the rights of Hong Kong citizens

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:56.060
and would instruct immigration officials not to punish visa applicants.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:59.080
who've been arrested for participating in the protests.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:03.400
With America already in a trade war with China such a move could escalate tensions

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.680
but advocates say it would be easy for China to comply.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:07.980
The Hong Kong issue is quite different

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:09.180
from the trade war.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:10.480
The Hong Kong issue

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.320
The Hong Kong issue is actually preventable.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:15.620
After the U.S. passes the law

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.620
it's up to China to decide.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:20.940
They can just do what they did in the past.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:24.880
They do not have to tighten control on Hong Kong.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:31.100
At the moment I have to say
that I'm pretty pessimistic

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:41.680
because it's just so difficult to stop China from extending the kind of power and control that they wish for.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:46.380
particularly with the current leader of the country, Xi Jinping,

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:52.880
he seems to be a very much of a
total control type of mentality type of person.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.180
In a way I think many of us are
probably even thinking that

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:03.340
what we're trying to do right now is to slow down
the change in the wrong direction

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:08.030
preserving whatever we've got through the one country, two system at the moment.

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:11.820
Despite that pessimism the movements
leaders say they have no choice

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:18.680
but to keep fighting for liberty and democracy or else Hong Kong will be subsumed into an authoritarian state.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.360
There's only win or not win

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.360
If we lose,

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:25.080
we lose a generation.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.480
This generation, they will be

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:28.760
put into jail

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:31.000
they will be harassed.

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:32.760
So all or nothing.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.920
And now or never.

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:37.180
There is a Cantonese saying

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:40.740
that this is the end game of the Hong Kong people.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:43.080
If they do not take to the streets

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:45.340
they will have no future for the next generation.

